.TH XM2089 6 "October 2026" "2089 0.1.0" "Games"
.SH NAME
xm2089 \- the game of 2048, for Motif, with or without CDE
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B xm2089
[
.I toolkit options
] [
.B \-mono
] [
.B \-colors
.I scheme
] [
.B \-extended
|
.B +extended
] [
.B \-arrows
] [
.B \-u
] [
.B \-s
.I size
] [
.B \-r
.I seed
] [
.B \-l
.I savestring
] [
.I file
]
.br
.B xm2089
.B \-v
|
.B \-h
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B xm2089
is the game of 2048 as a Motif-native application.
It runs on its own under any window manager, and integrates with the
CDE desktop where there is one (see
.BR "WITHOUT CDE" ,
below).
The rules are those of
.BR 2089 (6):
slide the tiles, join equal ones, and reach 2048 to win, then keep going
if you like until no move is left.
.PP
The window has a menu bar, the score, best score and moves in a row of
sunken fields, the board, New Game and Undo buttons, and a status line.
The board fills the window and follows it when it is resized; the
window can't be made smaller than its contents need.
Tiles are raised with Motif's bevels.
Arrow buttons to play with the mouse can be shown under the board
(View > Arrow Buttons): a click on one in a window behind others brings
2089 forward and moves at once.
.PP
Every new game has a number, shown on the status line.
The same number and size always deal the same game, here or in the
other 2089 programs.
.SH MENUS
.TP
.B File
New Game, New Game from Number, Open (a saved game), Save As (a file),
Save String (to copy, or to paste another and play it), Exit.
.TP
.B Edit
Undo, if undo is on.
.TP
.B View
Arrow Buttons; Best Scores, for each board size, with the top tile of
each, and buttons to clear one size or all of them.
.TP
.B Options
Board Size, from 3 x 3 to 8 x 8, which starts a new game (asking first
if one is under way); Game Colors, Desktop Colors or Black and White;
Extended Colors; Save Options.
.TP
.B Help
Overview (the help volume, under CDE), Keys, Using Help, About.
.SS Colors
.B Game Colors
are the original game's, from 2 to 2048, and past 2048 greens, blues
and violets: the Extended Colors.
Turn those off, and every tile past 2048 is the original's one dark
color, which also leaves their colors to other programs on a display
that has few.
.B Desktop Colors
are the colors of the CDE palette chosen in Style Manager, read when
2089 starts.
.B Black and White
is for monochrome displays, which get nothing else.
The rest of the window always takes the desktop's colors and fonts.
.SH OPTIONS
.B xm2089
takes the X Toolkit's options:
.BR \-display ,
.BR \-geometry ,
.BR \-name ,
.B \-xrm
and the rest.
Its own are:
.TP
.B \-mono
Black and White.
.TP
.BI \-colors " scheme"
The board's colors:
.B game
(the default),
.B desktop
or
.BR mono .
.TP
.B \-extended
.PD 0
.TP
.B +extended
.PD
Extended Colors on (the default) or off.
.TP
.B \-arrows
Show the arrow buttons.
.TP
.B \-u
Allow undo.
The desktop's action starts 2089 with it.
.TP
.BI \-s " size"
A board
.I size
cells across, from 2 to 8.
The default is 4.
.TP
.BI \-r " seed"
Play game number
.IR seed ,
from 0 to 4294967295.
.TP
.BI \-l " savestring"
Carry on a saved game.
.TP
.I file
Open a saved game.
.TP
.B \-v
Print the version.
.TP
.B \-h
Print a summary of the options.
.SH KEYS
Type on the board.
.TP
.BR "w a s d" , " k h j l" , " 8 4 2 6" ", or the arrow keys"
Move up, left, down or right.
.TP
.B u
Undo the last move, if undo is on.
.TP
.B n
Start a new game, after asking.
.TP
.B p
Show the save string.
.TP
.B c
The next color scheme.
.TP
.B ?
The keys.
.TP
.B q
Exit.
.SH RESOURCES
Application resources, for class
.BR XM2089 :
.TP
.BR undo " (Boolean, false)"
Allow undo.
.TP
.BR size " (Int, 4)"
The board size of new games.
.TP
.BR colors " (String, game)"
.BR game ,
.B desktop
or
.BR mono .
.TP
.BR mono " (Boolean, false)"
Black and White.
.TP
.BR extended " (Boolean, true)"
Extended Colors.
.TP
.BR arrows " (Boolean, false)"
Show the arrow buttons.
.PP
Options > Save Options writes the board size, the colors and the arrow
buttons to
.B ~/.xm2089
as resources, for the next time 2089 starts.
These win over the user's other resources
.RB ( ~/.Xdefaults ,
or what
.BR xrdb (1)
loaded), and the command line wins over them.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B ~/.2089
The best score for each board size, shared with
.BR 2089 (6)
and
.BR x2089 (6):
one save string a line, the game as it stood at its best.
.TP
.B ~/.xm2089
Saved options.
.TP
.IB name .2089
A saved game (File > Save As): its save string, on one line.
.SH WITHOUT CDE
.B xm2089
needs Motif 2.0 or later and any window manager:
.BR mwm (1)
or
.BR dtwm (1),
or another.
Without a CDE session, Desktop Colors is not offered (the palette comes
from CDE's session manager); Help > Overview and Using Help need CDE's
help viewer, and show the keys where it is missing; and the window's
icon is the window manager's own.
Everything else is the same.
Built against Motif 1.2, it lacks Desktop Colors altogether.
.SH THE CDE DESKTOP
Installed with
.BR "make install-cde" ,
2089 is in Application Manager, in a Games group; saved games open from
File Manager; and Help > Overview opens its help volume,
.BR 2089 .
The game being played is saved with the session when you log out, and
comes back when you log in.
.PP
On quitting from a terminal,
.B xm2089
prints the command that carries on the game, as
.BR 2089 (6)
does.
